Function losespells in src/spell.c is quoted below, and detabified for
your protection:

void
losespells()
{
        boolean confused = (Confusion != 0);
        int  n, nzap, i;

        book = 0;
        for (n = 0; n < MAXSPELL && spellid(n) != NO_SPELL; n++)
                continue;
        if (n) {
                nzap = rnd(n) + confused ? 1 : 0;
                if (nzap > n) nzap = n;
                for (i = n - nzap; i < n; i++) {
                    spellid(i) = NO_SPELL;
                    exercise(A_WIS, FALSE);     /* ouch! */
                }
        }
}

Compiling with clang -Wall raises a warning on the line
        nzap = rnd(n) + confused ? 1 : 0;
and rightly so:  the + operator has higher precedence than the ?: operator.
Furthermore, rnd(n) returns at least 1.  The expression rnd(n) + confused
is never zero, regardless of n and regardless of whether you were confused;
and nzap is always set to 1, so you always lose exactly one spell, assuming
that you know at least one.

The corresponding lines in NetHack 3.1.3 read:
        nzap = rnd(n);
        if (nzap < n) nzap += confused;
and this will indeed do what the Devteam appears to have intended:  lose
1dN spells, where N is how many spells you know, and one more if confused.

Examining the leaked beta shows that the bug is fixed -- but purely by
accident.  The loss count is changed to rn2(n+1), with confusion causing a
second roll and the higher of the two chosen, and Luck can reduce the
count.  A lengthy comment, however, indicates that the Devteam still thought
that nzap could be something other than one.  This bug was introduced in
NetHack 3.2.0, released in 1996, and the Devteam never knew it was there.

That same comment notes that the lost spells are always from the end of the
list, and the player can change the order.  The beta changes the logic so
that the lost spells can come from anywhere on the list.

Clearly, a set of parentheses is needed:
        nzap = rnd(n) + (confused ? 1 : 0);
and I propose to add this to my bugfix repository[1].  Perhaps some code
needs to be patched in from the beta as well, to allow any spell to be lost;
I don't propose changing the formula for nzap, as I intend the bugfix
repository for bugfixes rather than new features.

